<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel II Palaiologos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1350">1350</a>–1425</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel II Palaiologos was Byzantine emperor from 1391 to 1425. Earlier, as ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, he retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. As emperor he endured an Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> seeking help, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> in 1402 gave the empire a reprieve. His wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helena-dragas">Helena Dragaš</a> was the mother of the last emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-ii-palaiologos.jpg" alt="Manuel II Palaiologos" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Pitchka · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_II_Palaiologos_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel was born on 27 June 1350, the second son of Emperor John V Palaiologos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helena-kantakouzene">Helena Kantakouzene</a>. His father gave him the title of despotes, and he travelled west to seek support for the empire in 1365 and 1370, serving as governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> from 1369. After his older brother Andronikos IV attempted a usurpation in 1373, Manuel was proclaimed his father's heir and emperor on 25 September 1373.</p><p>Andronikos and then his son John VII displaced Manuel and his father in 1376 to 1379 and again in 1390, but Manuel defeated his nephew in 1390 with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the Ottoman Sultan Bayezid I. Bayezid then kept him at Prousa as an honorary hostage and made him join the campaign that took Philadelpheia, the last Byzantine enclave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On hearing in February 1391 of his father's death, Manuel fled the Ottoman court and secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> against John VII. In 1392 he was married and crowned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helena-dragas">Helena Dragaš</a>, daughter of the fellow Ottoman vassal Constantine Dragaš. Bayezid blockaded Constantinople from 1394 to 1402. He wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a>, who sent Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-ii-le-maingre">Jean II Le Maingre</a> with six ships and 1,200 men.</p><p>After some five years of siege, Manuel left his nephew John VII in charge of the city with a French garrison and sailed on 10 December 1399. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in April 1400, met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and Charles VI at Charenton on 3 June 1400. In December he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where Henry IV received him at Blackheath on 21 December, and he stayed at Eltham Palace until mid-February 1401, making him the only Byzantine emperor to visit England.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The defeat of Bayezid by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a> in 1402 relieved the pressure. In the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>, John VII recovered the European shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, and later returned the capital to Manuel.</p><p>A stroke paralysed him on 1 October 1422, though his mind was unaffected. He became a monk with the name Matthew and died on 21 July 1425, aged 75, and was buried at the Pantokrator Monastery. His sons John VIII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a> became emperors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel II Palaiologos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Byzantium under <b>Manuel II</b> then retook Serres, which fell to the Ottomans in 1383.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-serres">Despotate of Serres</a></li><li><blockquote>Konstantin's daughter Helena married the Byzantine emperor <b>Manuel II</b> and was the mother of the last emperor, Constantine XI.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-dejanovic">Lordship of Dejanović</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1398" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Manuel II Palaiologos"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1398">What was here in 1398? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
